<Site
  title="About · Ouijit"
  description="Ouijit is built by one person, Prentice Bjerkeseth. Free, open source under AGPL, no account and no telemetry, and the plan is to keep it that way.">
  <main class="compare-content about-content">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      Ouijit is a desktop app for running several coding agents at once, each in its own terminal and git
      worktree. It's for experienced engineers who carry a lot of scope and want to run more of it in parallel,
      who care how their tools look and feel, and who want sandboxing that is local and convenient. It isn't
      for anyone after a tmux-style wall of panes or a traditional editor.
    </p>
    <p>
      The more agents you run, the more there is to read and decide, and a tool like this should protect your
      focus and your judgement. Most of the design work goes there. Terminals sit in a stack instead of a busy
      grid, each project carries its own avatar so you always know where you are, and the git plumbing of
      branches and worktrees stays out of the way of the work. The interactions are tactile on purpose.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ouijit is free and open source under AGPL-3.0, with no account and no telemetry. If it ever charges for
      something, it will be a feature with a running cost of its own, such as hosted environments or sync. The
      licence keeps the code open and makes it hard for a company to take the work and sell it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I build Ouijit alone, and I build it with Ouijit. The <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a> shows how often
      it ships. Many tools like this are raising venture money, getting acquired, or shutting down, and I intend
      to keep this one independent. Bugs and requests go to
      <a href="https://github.com/ouijit/ouijit/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="about-sign"><a href="https://github.com/pbjer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prentice Bjerkeseth</a></p>
  </main>
</Site>
